---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { base } from '../../../utils/base.js';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import biomeMap from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-biomes.png';
import foldBeforeAfter from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-fold-before-after.png';
import foldDiagnostic from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-fold-diagnostic.png';
import snowMassBalance from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-snow-massbalance.png';
import streamThresholdRejected from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-stream-threshold-rejected.png';
---
<BaseLayout title="World-building: Latency — Technical Deep Dive - Nico">
  <div class="page-container">
    <div class="case-study">
      <header class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Case Study — World-building: Latency</p>
        <h1>Technical Deep Dive</h1>
        <p class="intro">
          This page documents the six-stage GIS pipeline behind the
          <a class="inline-link" href={`${base}/en/case-study-worldbuilding/`}> World-building case study </a>
          in full: the exact generation and analysis method for each stage,
          the parameters used, the validation performed against a real
          reference region, and — deliberately included rather than
          cropped out — the bugs found along the way and how they were
          diagnosed. Latency is a fictional island, so nothing here is
          "ground truth" in the way Torino's is; the standard applied
          instead is internal physical/statistical consistency and, where
          possible, comparison against a real analog region.
        </p>
        <p class="aside">
          Validation reference throughout: South Island, New Zealand
          (Southern Alps) — a young, actively-uplifting island range with a
          strong orographic rain shadow, similar latitude magnitude, and
          four real seasons.
        </p>
      </header>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <section class="methodology">

        <article class="step">
          <h2>1. Domain, CRS, and pipeline architecture</h2>
          <p>
            Everything in this project lives on the same 130&nbsp;km
            (E–W) × 160&nbsp;km (N–S) domain, in a custom Lambert Conformal
            Conic projection defined for this fictional world rather than
            reused from a real place:
          </p>
          <pre class="spec-block">+proj=lcc +lat_1=-44.48 +lat_2=-43.52 +lat_0=-44 +lon_0=42 +x_0=0 +y_0=0 +datum=WGS84 +units=m +no_defs
xmin=-65000  xmax=65000  ymin=-80000  ymax=80000   (meters)</pre>
          <p>
            Six sequential stages, each closed out with a written decision
            log before the next began: <strong>terrain</strong> (30&nbsp;m) →
            <strong>climate</strong> (temperature + precipitation, 120&nbsp;m
            working resolution) → <strong>snow &amp; derived climate
            metrics</strong> → <strong>hydrology</strong> (30&nbsp;m) →
            <strong>biomes</strong> (120&nbsp;m) → <strong>settlement
            suitability</strong> (120&nbsp;m). Every stage reads only the
            previous stages' outputs — no stage was allowed to reach back
            and quietly patch an earlier one; when an earlier assumption
            turned out wrong (see the DEM fold bug below, or the naive vs.
            mass-balance snow line in section 4), it's recorded as a
            correction, not silently absorbed.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>No GRASS/GDAL/rasterio/shapely for most of this
            project.</strong> The working sandbox this pipeline was built
            in had restricted package access, so tools normally reached for
            first — <code>r.watershed</code>, <code>r.stream.extract</code>,
            <code>r.grow.distance</code>, geometry libraries — were
            reimplemented from their published algorithms in
            NumPy/SciPy/scikit-image instead of assumed available. That
            constraint shows up repeatedly below.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>2. Terrain generation <span class="step-ref">(Tappa 1)</span></h2>
          <p>
            Elevation is <code>structure(x,y) + noise(x,y) × amplitude −
            sea_level_offset</code>. <strong>Structure</strong> comes from a
            hand-authored skeleton (ridge centerlines with Gaussian
            cross-sections, plateau/amplitude zones) rather than emerging
            from noise alone — the mountain spine, its branching, and the
            regional plains are drawn, not rolled. <strong>Noise</strong> is
            a ridged/turbulence Simplex fBm (signed fold
            <code>1 − 2·|n|</code>) with domain warping (Iñigo Quílez-style
            pseudo-random vector field, two octave bands — a coastline-scale
            warp and a fine-detail warp) added for irregularity.
          </p>
          <pre class="spec-block">seed: 42                          noise_octaves: 6
noise_base_wavelength_m: 25000    noise_amplitude_m: 200
lacunarity: 2.0                   persistence: 0.5
warp_wavelength_m: 15000          warp_amplitude_m: 5000
noise_warp_wavelength_m: 4000     noise_warp_amplitude_m: 1500</pre>
          <p>
            <strong>Hydraulic erosion:</strong> droplet-based (Beyer/Lague
            method), fluvial only — 300,000 droplets for the initial pass,
            later densified to 0.3 droplets per land cell (~3.2M droplets)
            once the DEM was finalized.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Real-texture grafting (v2).</strong> Pure synthetic
            noise measured as spectrally too smooth against real terrain: a
            2D FFT radial power-spectral-density analysis gave a spectral
            slope β≈2.50 (long wavelengths) rising to β≈4.54 (short
            wavelengths) for real SRTM data, versus a flat β≈3.72–3.74 for
            the synthetic noise at every wavelength — visually, "rougher
            terrain reads as smoother than it should." Fix: real SRTM GL3
            texture (Piemonte/western Alps, detrended with a 15&nbsp;km
            Gaussian blur to remove its own regional slope) was grafted in
            as the fine-detail layer, closing the gap to β≈2.38 / β≈4.03.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Declared inconsistency:</strong> the project's own
            validation reference is South Island, NZ, but the texture graft
            uses Piemonte SRTM data. An actual South Island tile was tested
            first — 39.7% NODATA overall, and even its cleanest low-void
            window (near Lake Hawea/Wanaka) produced a "dead-straight
            valley/lake gash" once grafted onto the skeleton (glacial-lake
            troughs don't generalize as texture). Piemonte was used
            instead, and the substitution is stated here rather than
            hidden — the two regions' spectral slopes were checked as
            broadly comparable (β≈2.87/4.91 vs. NZ's β≈3.12/5.09, not
            directly comparable since measured with a different script) but
            this is <em>not</em> a rigorous equivalence claim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="substep">The domain-warp fold bug</h3>
          <p>
            QGIS review of the v2 DEM's low-relief plains flagged a texture
            that read as "coral or biological," not terrain. The domain
            warp folds onto itself once the underlying vector field's
            Jacobian determinant goes negative — mathematically, the warp
            stops being a 1-to-1 remapping and starts multiply-covering
            some areas of the output while leaving gaps in others. Root
            cause: <code>persistence(0.7) × lacunarity(1.75) = 1.225</code>
            — greater than 1, meaning higher noise octaves were gaining
            weight instead of decaying, compounding the fold. Measured at
            100&nbsp;m sampling: <strong>35.1% of the entire domain</strong>
            had a folded warp map.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={foldBeforeAfter}
              alt="Whole-domain fold map before and after the warp-amplitude fix: 35.1% of cells folded (left) versus 1.3% (right)"
              widths={[600, 1200, 1800]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 58rem"
              format="webp"
              quality={82}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Cells with a folded (Jacobian ≤ 0) domain warp, 100 m sampling, whole 130×160 km domain — before (left, current v2 warp) and after (right, warp amplitude scaled to 40%)</figcaption>
          <p>
            Confirmed independently three ways before trusting the
            diagnosis: a direct visual overlay of the fold-cell mask against
            the "coral" artifact in an actual low-relief DEM crop (matching
            essentially cell-for-cell), the whole-domain fold map on its
            own with no elevation data at all (already a dense maze/coral
            pattern with no other input), and closed circular ring
            artifacts visible in eroded hillshade crops — a shape real
            drainage networks never produce, since a river network is a
            tree, not a closed loop.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={foldDiagnostic}
              alt="Side-by-side comparison of an 18 km DEM crop showing coral-like terrain artifacts and the corresponding fold-cell mask, matching almost cell-for-cell"
              widths={[600, 1200, 1800]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 58rem"
              format="webp"
              quality={82}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Same 18 km crop of the North Plains: the visible "coral" artifact in the v2 DEM (left) directly overlaid against the fold-cell mask alone (right)</figcaption>
          <p>
            <strong>Fix:</strong> scaled both warp amplitudes to 40% of
            their v2 values (<code>warp_amplitude_m: 5000→2000</code>,
            <code>noise_warp_amplitude_m: 1500→600</code>; wavelengths,
            lacunarity, and persistence unchanged). Fold fraction dropped
            from 35.1% to 1.3% domain-wide (north plains: 40.3%→2.2%; peak
            zone: 39.2%→2.1%; coastline: 39.1%→1.8%), while total land
            fraction barely moved (27.9%→28.0% for the main island). This
            is the current, active DEM (v3).
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Declared limitation:</strong> residual fold is
            "1.3–2.2%, not mathematically zero." The coastline also reads
            as somewhat more regular/"blobby" post-fix than v2's (unsafe)
            pre-fix coastline — a deliberate trade-off, not resolved this
            stage. A numeric plausibility check of the final DEM against
            the actual South Island NZ reference (beyond the qualitative
            spectral comparison above) was flagged as worth doing but not
            attempted in this stage.
          </p>
          <p class="map-source">
            Final v3 DEM: 30 m resolution, 5,334 × 4,334 grid, elevation
            range −945.6 to +4020.2 m, land fraction 45.3% (later corrected
            to 47.6% once Tappa 2 converted all <code>amplitude_zone</code>s
            to <code>plateau</code>, see section 3).
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>3. Climate: temperature and precipitation <span class="step-ref">(Tappa 2)</span></h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Temperature</strong> is a closed-form model: a
            sea-level field with its own seasonal cosine cycle, reduced by
            elevation with a lapse rate that itself varies monthly.
          </p>
          <pre class="spec-block">T(x,y,m) = T_sl(x,y,m) − γ(m) · z(x,y)
T_sl(x,y,m) = T_ref + dT/dlat·(lat(y) − lat_ref) + A(x,y)·cos(2π(m − m_peak(x,y))/12)

lapse_rate_c_per_km: 5.0            (Norton 1985, 301 NZ stations — not the ICAO 6.5 standard)
lapse_seasonal_amplitude_c_per_km: 0.3   peak month: February
t_ref_sea_level_c: 11.5             lat_ref_deg: -44.0
amplitude_coastal_c: 4.0            amplitude_inland_c: 7.0
continentality_scale_km: 30.0       (SciPy distance_transform_edt, standing in for r.grow.distance)</pre>
          <p>
            <strong>Precipitation</strong> uses Smith &amp; Barstad's (2004)
            linear orographic-precipitation theory, solved in Fourier
            space — moist air is advected over the terrain spectrum, with
            condensation/fallout delay time constants and a moist Brunt–
            Väisälä stability frequency shaping where rain falls relative to
            the ridge crest.
          </p>
          <pre class="spec-block">P̂(k,l) = Cw · iσ · ĥ(k,l) / [(1 − imHw)(1 + iστc)(1 + iστf)]

wind direction: 250° (WSW), locked      wind speed: 12.0 m/s ± 2.0 seasonal
Nm (moist stability): 0.005 s⁻¹         τc = τf: 1000 s
orographic_duty_cycle: 0.07             (calibration factor — raw model output was ~170,000 mm/yr uncalibrated)
background_precip_mm_per_month: 60.0    lee_floor_fraction: 0.45</pre>
          <p>
            Wind direction was tested, not assumed: effectiveness at 44°S
            was checked for W (270°, 0.65), WSW (250°, 0.77 — adopted), and
            SW (230°, 0.81 but rejected as hard to justify at this latitude
            and it weakened the southern branch of the range too much
            relative to the narrative).
          </p>
          <table class="spec-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Latency</th><th>South Island, NZ reference</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Windward lowland precipitation</td><td>2,402 mm/yr</td><td>Greymouth, 2,443 mm/yr</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Leeward lowland precipitation</td><td>430 mm/yr</td><td>Christchurch, 612 mm/yr</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Windward:leeward ratio</td><td>5.6:1</td><td>4.0:1</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Domain max precipitation</td><td>12,320 mm/yr</td><td>Southern Alps crest, ~11,000–13,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Coastal annual mean temperature</td><td>11.3°C</td><td>Hokitika/Christchurch, 11.7–12.2°C</td></tr>
              <tr><td>0°C isotherm, coldest month</td><td>1,410 m</td><td>Southern Alps winter, ~1,200–1,600 m</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Where it diverges, and why:</strong> the windward:leeward
            ratio and inland temperature range both run higher than the
            real reference. Attributed to geography, not model error — this
            island is smaller and steeper (mean elevation 919 m vs. the
            real island's ~500 m), with a narrower lee band and nowhere
            more than 30 km from the sea, all of which sharpen an
            orographic contrast that's already strong in the real target.
            The model itself also carries no moisture budget (rain only
            decays leeward within the domain — a second downwind range
            would never be starved) and the terrain's non-dimensional
            mountain height sits near the flow-blocking threshold, which
            likely overstates crest precipitation and understates the
            upwind flank — a flow-blocking correction was identified as a
            future extension, not applied here.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>4. Snow and derived climate metrics <span class="step-ref">(Tappa 3)</span></h2>
          <p>
            Three products, all derived from Tappa 2's monthly stacks
            without adding new spatial input. <strong>Months with
            snow</strong> uses a normal-CDF rain/snow partition (not a hard
            0°C cutoff) — <code>t50_snow_rain_c = 1.0°C</code> (Jennings et
            al. 2018, ~17,000 real stations) with a
            <code>σ_day_c = 3.0°C</code> daily-variability spread, a month
            counting only once modelled snow water equivalent clears 5 mm.
            <strong>Seasonality index</strong> is just warmest-minus-coldest
            monthly mean, land range 7.60–10.72°C.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Permanent snow line</strong> replaced Tappa 2's
            provisional "all twelve monthly means below 0°C" rule (already
            flagged there as sitting above the real equilibrium line) with
            a minimal annual mass-balance model: accumulation from modelled
            snowfall, ablation from a positive-degree-day melt model
            (<code>degree_day_factor = 4.5 mm/°C·day</code>, Hock 2003).
            "Permanent" = accumulation − melt ≥ 0 for the year.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={snowMassBalance}
              alt="Three-panel snow figure: months with modelled snow, seasonality index, and permanent snow extent comparing the naive temperature-only threshold against the mass-balance model"
              widths={[600, 1200, 1800]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 58rem"
              format="webp"
              quality={82}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Right panel: permanent-snow extent under the naive T-only rule (blue, 108 km²) versus the mass-balance model (blue + orange, 960 km²) — a ~9× correction, in the direction Tappa 2 had already predicted</figcaption>
          <table class="spec-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Latency</th><th>Real Southern Alps</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>ELA, windward/wet</td><td>2,151 m</td><td>~1,600 m (west of the Main Divide)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>ELA, leeward/dry</td><td>3,288 m</td><td>~2,000–2,200 m (east)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Windward–leeward ELA differential</td><td>1,136 m</td><td>400–600 m</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Direction right, magnitude off — and that's informative,
            not just a caveat.</strong> The ELA differential runs 2–3×
            larger than the real reference, echoing Tappa 2's own overstated
            windward:leeward precipitation ratio — the same geographic
            cause propagating downstream through two independent models. A
            sensitivity check (re-running with
            <code>lapse_seasonal_amplitude_c_per_km</code> forced to 0)
            found the naive metric swung 88% on that one uncalibrated
            parameter, while the mass-balance metric moved only 8% — an
            unplanned but real robustness gain from switching methods, not
            just a number that happened to come out closer to reality.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>5. Hydrology <span class="step-ref">(Tappa 4)</span></h2>
          <p>
            No GRASS available, so <code>r.watershed</code>/
            <code>r.stream.extract</code> were reimplemented from their
            published algorithm: Priority-Flood + Epsilon (Barnes, Lehman
            &amp; Mulla 2014) fills depressions and resolves D8 flow
            direction in a single min-heap pass seeded from ocean cells,
            at the DEM's native 30 m (5,334 × 4,334, 23.1M cells) — this
            stage works at full terrain resolution, not the 120 m
            climate/biome grid. A reverse topological pass over the fill
            order accumulates flow, computed twice: a plain cell count and
            a precipitation-weighted version using Tappa 2's actual monthly
            rain, so seasonal streamflow could later be derived without
            re-running the routing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Streams are extracted at a plain contributing-area threshold
            (<code>0.3 km²</code>), calibrated by visually sweeping
            0.1/0.5/2.0 km² test crops and cross-checked against Horton's
            (1945) drainage-density rule of thumb.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={streamThresholdRejected}
              alt="Four candidate area-times-slope-squared channel initiation thresholds, each flooding wide areas of steep terrain solid blue instead of isolating a thin stream network"
              widths={[600, 1200, 1800]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 58rem"
              format="webp"
              quality={82}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Rejected: area × slope² channel-initiation (Montgomery &amp; Foufoula-Georgiou, 1993). On this DEM's steepest terrain the criterion floods whole hillslopes rather than isolating channels — the formulation needs local calibration against real channel-head field survey data this project doesn't have</figcaption>
          <table class="spec-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Latency</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Drainage density</td><td>1.14 km/km²</td><td>Horton (1945) "moderate" range: 1–3 km/km²</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Max discharge proxy / area</td><td>0.137 m³/s/km²</td><td>Rule-of-thumb range: 0.03–0.15</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Windward:leeward discharge ratio</td><td>5.9:1</td><td>Tracks Tappa 2's 5.6:1 precipitation ratio</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Two bugs caught by physical implausibility, not by
            code review.</strong> A precision collision — reconstructing
            flow order from a saved float32 elevation grid instead of
            re-running the fill in float64 — silently collapsed 21,944
            cells to identical elevations (float32's ~0.001 m precision vs.
            the algorithm's own 1e-4 m tie-break epsilon), dragging
            correlation against the trusted discharge raster to 0.87.
            Separately, monthly discharge maxima came out an order of
            magnitude below the annual maximum (6.7–11.9 vs. 103.1 m³/s) —
            physically backwards — traced to dividing by seconds-per-year
            instead of seconds-per-month in the seasonal routing pass. Both
            declared limitations, not silently patched: discharge is stated
            throughout as "an upper bound, not a discharge model" — no
            infiltration, evapotranspiration, or channel routing lag is
            modeled.
          </p>
          <p class="map-source">
            Final: 9,912.5 km² land area, 377,281 stream cells (~11,318 km
            of channel), 196,094 lake-mask cells (176.5 km², 1.8% of land,
            flagged as an unreviewed byproduct of the fill pass rather than
            a curated lake catalogue).
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>6. Biome classification <span class="step-ref">(Tappa 5)</span></h2>
          <p>
            A two-axis Holdridge Life Zone classification (Holdridge,
            1967) — biotemperature and a moisture (PET ratio) axis —
            replacing the original 2025 attempt's single-axis,
            temperature-only limitation. PET is derived from biotemperature
            via Holdridge's own empirical constant
            (<code>PET = 58.93 × biotemperature</code>); the temperature
            belt uses Holdridge's standard bands (1.5/3/6/12/18/24°C).
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Holdridge's own absolute moisture bands don't work on
            this world.</strong> Nothing on the island cleared Holdridge's
            "Semiarid" threshold — even the driest cell (324 mm/yr) still
            registered as "Subhumid" on his scale, because PET itself caps
            near 700 mm/yr when biotemperature never exceeds 12°C here.
            Fix: the moisture axis is rebinned into <strong>terciles of
            this world's own land-cell distribution</strong> (wet &lt;
            0.1184, moist 0.1184–0.5360, dry &gt; 0.5360) instead of
            Holdridge's real-world-calibrated absolute cutoffs — a
            world-specific classification grounded in the same theory, not
            a literal transplant of Earth's numbers.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={biomeMap}
              alt="Biome classification overlay on hillshade, showing the dry-scrub band clearly wider on the northeast lee side than the southwest windward side"
              widths={[600, 1200, 1800]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 58rem"
              format="webp"
              quality={82}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Final classification, 120 m resolution, 9 classes, overlaid on hillshade</figcaption>
          <p>
            The strongest single confirmation of the wind-direction
            decision propagating cleanly through every downstream stage:
            <strong>Subalpine Dry Scrub is 335.1 km² on the leeward side and
            0.0 km² windward</strong> — a clean, complete separation, not a
            gradient.
          </p>
          <table class="spec-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Area</th><th>% of domain</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Lowland Steppe / Grassland</td><td>2,962.2 km²</td><td>29.9%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Woodland / Shrubland</td><td>2,834.8 km²</td><td>28.6%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Permanent Snow &amp; Ice</td><td>960.2 km²</td><td>9.7%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Subalpine Wet Forest</td><td>942.4 km²</td><td>9.5%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Temperate Forest</td><td>689.8 km²</td><td>7.0%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Alpine Tundra</td><td>616.5 km²</td><td>6.2%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Subalpine Dry Scrub</td><td>335.1 km²</td><td>3.4%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Alpine Fellfield</td><td>396.0 km²</td><td>4.0%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Subalpine Woodland</td><td>167.2 km²</td><td>1.7%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Declared limitation:</strong> the published color
            palette fails strict colorblind-safety on its worst pair
            (Temperate Forest vs. Subalpine Wet Forest, ΔE 5.6 against a
            15.0 floor) — mitigated only through the interactive map's
            click-to-select panel and legend, not fixed at the color level.
            The 58.93 PET constant was also fit mostly on tropical/
            subtropical stations and is "numerically fragile" below ~3°C
            biotemperature — a live concern given this world's 7.39°C land
            mean.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>7. Settlement suitability and site selection <span class="step-ref">(Tappa 6)</span></h2>
          <p>
            Two layer types, kept architecturally separate: <strong>núcleo</strong>
            layers (continuous 0–1 desirability, combined by a
            population-specific weighted sum) and <strong>exclusão</strong>
            layers (0–1 multipliers on the composite, never additive terms).
            Six núcleo layers — slope, water proximity, agriculture (a TWI
            proxy), solar exposure, biome suitability, and settlement size
            read off downstream rather than weighted in — feed two
            <em>separate</em> composite surfaces, not one shared index,
            because the Círculo (open-field agriculture) and Povo Livre
            (forestry/foraging) economies weight biome so differently that
            a single blended index would misrepresent one of them.
          </p>
          <table class="spec-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Círculo weight</th><th>Povo Livre weight</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Biome</td><td>0.30</td><td>0.60</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agriculture (TWI)</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.10</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Slope</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.10</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Solar exposure</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.15</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Water proximity</td><td>0.05</td><td>0.05</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>
            <strong>Solar exposure</strong> is a from-scratch FAO-56
            elevation-corrected clear-sky model (no <code>r.sun</code>
            available), integrated over 16 horizon directions with a 10 km
            shading radius across 12 mid-month days. It reproduced a
            genuine, checked physical result for this world's southern
            hemisphere: north-facing slopes receive ~37% more annual
            insolation than south-facing slopes at matched grade (8,578 vs.
            6,257 MJ/m²/yr, 8–25° slopes) — the mirror-image of the
            northern-hemisphere intuition, confirmed rather than assumed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Site selection</strong> (Círculo surface only, 17
            sites): population converts to footprint via an assumed density
            of 4,000 people/km², candidate cells are screened by mean
            suitability over a square window, and sites are placed greedily,
            largest population first, irrevocably. Two independent
            minimum-distance rules apply simultaneously: straight-line
            footprint non-overlap, and a cost-distance (Tobler hiking
            function + 6.0 km/h boat speed) minimum travel time between
            same-tier pairs and large–medium pairs.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Three redesign rounds before this held up.</strong> The
            first straight-line-only distance rule collapsed to 5 of 17
            sites placed (one site landed in a near-zero-suitability
            corner). A tier-scoped fix reached 17/17 but put every single
            site in the same biome — flagged, not shipped. The user's own
            proposed cost-distance model fixed the biome monoculture (12
            Grassland / 4 Woodland-Shrubland / 1 Temperate Forest) but a
            manual spot-check afterward still found 7 of 17 sites sitting
            partly in lakes (up to 95% of one large Círculo's window
            covered by lake water) and a medium site only ~4 km from a
            large one — caught by eye, not by any automated check, and
            fixed by excluding lake cells from the effective land mask and
            adding an explicit large–medium minimum-distance tier.
          </p>
          <p class="map-source">
            Final: 17/17 Círculos placed, 0 minimum-distance violations, 0
            on a lake — 4 large, 5 medium, 8 small. Mean suitability
            0.762–0.887 across all sites. Slope thresholds (5%/30% grade),
            the exclusion-zone buffer distances, and the settlement density
            figure are all stated as placeholders, not calibrated to any
            in-world source — there's no real soil/pedology data anywhere
            in this project, only a topographic-wetness proxy standing in
            for it.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>8. Interactive map — implementation notes</h2>
          <p>
            The interactive map (MapLibre GL JS, no third-party basemap
            tiles) reads directly from the pipeline's own rasters and
            vectors — biomes, rivers, lakes, the 17 Círculo sites, and four
            raster overlays (precipitation, temperature, months-with-snow,
            permanent snow). Since this world has no real latitude/
            longitude, every layer is registered in a shared "pseudo-degree"
            space: native LCC meters divided by a fixed
            <code>SCALE = 100,000</code>, applied identically everywhere so
            polygons, points, and raster image corners all land in the same
            coordinate frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Biome polygon topology.</strong> The first
            polygonization pass (marching-squares contours + a manual
            "smallest contour in this crop is probably a hole" heuristic)
            produced self-intersecting geometry, visible in MapLibre as
            triangular WebGL rendering glitches. Rebuilt with
            <code>cv2.findContours(mask, cv2.RETR_CCOMP)</code>, whose
            parent/child contour hierarchy is algorithmically guaranteed
            rather than guessed — this also fixed a second, opposite bug
            introduced by an intermediate "just drop all holes" fix: real
            interior enclaves (ocean bays cutting into a biome region) were
            being silently painted over solid instead of left open.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>River vectorization.</strong> The first attempt
            contoured the thresholded stream mask into fill polygons, then
            switched to a fixed-pixel-width line layer when those proved
            invisible — but rivers still didn't render. The real problem
            wasn't the layer type: a 30 m-wide stream corridor on a ~160 km
            domain is sub-pixel at any reasonable zoom regardless of how
            it's drawn, and contour-polygonizing that thin a mask fragments
            it into disconnected blobs (the largest was 645 m across, not a
            connected network). Fixed by skeletonizing the mask to a 1-pixel
            centerline and tracing continuous chains through a graph
            between junction/endpoint nodes — genuine topology instead of
            disconnected contour fragments. The published network is also
            filtered to a &gt;15 km² contributing-area threshold rather than
            the full &gt;3 km² network: topologically correct at the lower
            threshold, but visually dense enough to bury the biome layer
            underneath it.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Astro-specific gotcha, worth stating generally:</strong>
            a component's <code>&lt;style is:global&gt;</code> block is only
            bundled into a page that actually imports that component —
            sharing class names with another component on a different page
            doesn't pull in its styles. The map's CSS is fully
            self-contained for exactly this reason, not because it needed
            to diverge from the Torino map's design.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>9. Sources and methodology references</h2>
          <p>
            Nothing on this page is real-world published GIS data — the
            entire domain is synthetic/procedural. What follows are the
            published methods and real reference datasets the pipeline
            draws on for technique and validation, not for content:
          </p>
          <pre class="spec-block">Terrain texture (grafted detail):  SRTM GL3, Piemonte/western Alps
Erosion:                            Beyer / Lague droplet-based hydraulic erosion
Depression fill / flow routing:     Barnes, Lehman & Mulla (2014), Computers & Geosciences 62
Orographic precipitation:           Smith & Barstad (2004), J. Atmos. Sci. 61(12)
Lapse rate:                         Norton (1985); Franz Josef/Ben Ohau, NZ (Anderson et al. 2006; Doughty 2013)
Snow/rain partition:                Jennings et al. (2018), Nature Communications 9:1148
Degree-day melt factor:             Hock (2003), J. Hydrol. 282
Biome classification:               Holdridge (1967), Life Zone Ecology
Map projection math:                Snyder (1987), Map Projections — A Working Manual</pre>
        </article>

      </section>
    </div>

